## mk/flow.mk :: the post-target census is read by nothing
# Section 6 says the FPGA_POST_TARGET_VARS census makes a misspelt post-target
# variable "visible to `make check`". NOTHING READS IT outside `make env`'s last
# line - verified: the only occurrences are flow.mk:840 (comment), :915 (the
# export), :1552 (the print). A complete project with
# `BITSTREM_POST_TARGETS := deploy` gets `Contract complete.` and the deploy hook
# silently never runs. Carried as t_known_defect flow_mk.vars.post_census.checked
# - the census is asserted and proved; the CONSUMER is the marker, so it goes red
# the day the checker names it.
# Two smaller things measured beside it: section 7's claim that `$(.VARIABLES)`
# shows exactly six is false (twelve), and `dirs`' comment justifies its guard
# with "mkdir -p exits 0 when the path is a FILE" - GNU mkdir -p exits 1. The
# guard is right; its stated reason is not.

## flow/vivado/6_bitstream.tcl :: a post_bitstream hook can replace the .bit unrecorded
# `flow_hook post_bitstream` is at :462; the measurements that read `file size`
# start at :473; `prov_pin` is called only for routed_dcp (:172) and
# fpga_image_hex (:187). Verified: the .bit is NEVER pinned at write time, zero
# prov_pin calls name it. A hook that replaces the file is recorded as though
# write_bitstream wrote it. The template's warning that a post_bitstream hook
# "cannot change what ships" is true of the DESIGN and false of the FILE.
# WOULD SETTLE IT: cheap with t_bitstream's existing fixture - the stub knows the
# bytes it wrote, so an overwriting hook is detectable.

## flow/vivado/6_bitstream.tcl :: the firmware hash is recorded and compared by nothing
# Written at :493. ci/assert-stage.sh:562-567 only warns when it is ABSENT. The
# gate bullet at :596-599 says the synth manifest "is what proves which one was
# read" - and no code opens it. A bitstream carrying different firmware from the
# one synthesis saw is detectable by nothing in the flow.

## flow/steps/bitstream_opts.tcl :: the override contract is not the acceptance test
# The file tells an override, first and in capitals, that it MUST keep the
# CFGBVS/CONFIG_VOLTAGE section - "the whole reason this file is a step of its
# own". The acceptance test is BITSTREAM_OPTS_DONE plus the existence of three
# variable NAMES, none of which is that section. An override keeping the letter
# and dropping it is accepted in silence, and write_bitstream reports the absence
# as DRC CFGBVS-1, a WARNING - so the run exits 0 and every artefact assertion
# passes. Measured: bitstream_props comes out `(none)`.
# Carried as t_known_defect bit.override.config_bank.

## ci/deploy-gates.sh :: a discarded awk status makes the ARCHIVE lie
# awk exits non-zero when it cannot read its operand, and the gate file's
# hard-failure test reads that as "nothing found". Measured: verdict dir at mode
# 000 with deploy.verify failing -> the console reports the failing gate and
# reports/deploy_gate.txt says `HARD FAILURES: none`. Process exit is unaffected
# because ci_exit counts in memory, so CI keyed on status still goes red and ONLY
# THE ARCHIVED COPY LIES - the copy docs/DEPLOY.md sends readers to.
# Seven more carried as t_known_defect in t_deploy_gates.sh: --selftest grades its
# absent-record case on exit code alone, so a copy recording SKIP where the header
# promises UNVERIFIED passes its own selftest; deploy.test.log is read by the gate
# and written by nothing; a duplicate key is graded from the FIRST value though
# the driver documents last-write-wins; an absent deploy.program.skipped reads as
# "not skipped"; held_at_program=yes on a run whose acquire FAILED grades PASS on
# lease and SKIP on release in one file; deploy.preflight.group_holder is graded
# by nobody; and the driver records the holder it PROPOSED, never the hub's answer.

## the deploy tier has never touched a board
# scripts/fpga-flow-deploy (1390 lines), mk/deploy.mk and the fpgahub hooks have
# been exercised against fixtures and against the CLI's own dry-run paths.
# Nothing in this repository has ever programmed a device. Every claim about
# what happens when a lease is held, a program fails, or a board is already
# busy is a claim about code that has been read, not run.
# WOULD SETTLE IT: one deploy against a real KR260 with a lease, and one against
# a board that is already leased by somebody else.

## the part/board split is a one-implementation contract
# CONTRACT.md section 1 splits the reference toolkit's single `tech` concept into
# a toolkit-side part pack and a project-side board pack. Three part packs ship;
# one of them (xck26-sfvc784-2LV-c) has ever built anything. One board pack
# exists, in one project.
# An interface with one implementation is a description of that implementation.
# The reference toolkit's tech-pack API got two things wrong before it settled,
# and it settled by acquiring a second consumer.
# WOULD SETTLE IT: a second board on a different part, built end to end by
# somebody who did not write the pack API.

