## mk/flow.mk: 1709 lines, and only its parse-time guards are tested
# t_contract.sh covers the `$(error)` guards thoroughly - 13 planted faults
# rejected - because a make conditional that stops matching FAILS OPEN and the
# build just proceeds. Nothing covers the rest of the file: the stage graph, the
# ~70-variable surface, the ordering of the `?=` chains, or any recipe.
# That gap is why this file has NOT been split into per-area fragments, though
# it is past the length a reader can hold. Everything in a makefile interacts by
# expansion order, and three separate defects in this file during its first
# fortnight were exactly that: a `:=` that snapshotted before the includes ran,
# a rule whose target expanded at parse time and resolved to the filesystem
# root, and an `$(if)` whose prose contained a comma. A split moves every line
# in the file past those same hazards with 27 assertions standing behind it, for
# no functional gain, on the one component with demonstrated bitstream parity.
# WOULD SETTLE IT, in this order: a suite that drives `make -n` over the stage
# graph and asserts the prerequisite chain and the resolved variable surface;
# THEN the split. Not the other way round.

## ci/deploy-gates.sh is named by no test
# CLOSED for check-vendor-collateral.sh on 2026-09-17: test/shell/t_vendor_collateral.sh,
# 24 planted faults. It found FOUR defects in the checker that guards this PUBLIC
# repository against vendor IP, all carried as t_known_defect:
#
#   A SKIPPED SCAN EXITS 0, two independent ways. gawk is FATAL on an unreadable
#   operand and the scan discards both its stderr and its exit status, so one
#   mode-000 file hides every file sorting after it in its 400-file batch -
#   measured, with the census still reporting both as "read for content". And the
#   size pass matches `wc -c` output to its argument list BY POSITION; wc emits no
#   line for a file it cannot open, so every size shifts by one and the checker's
#   own "could not be measured" report is disarmed by an entry that is simply the
#   wrong file's.
#
#   THE PRE-COMMIT HOOK SILENTLY DROPS EVERY CONTENT FINDING. hooks/pre-commit
#   runs `--staged --fast --new-lines-only`; vc_addedlines calls `git diff`
#   WITHOUT --cached, so the added-line map describes HEAD-vs-worktree while the
#   scan reads the INDEX. When they differ every content finding in the staged
#   blob is filed as pre-existing backlog and dropped. Measured: `--staged --fast`
#   finds ip/core.v:2 and exits 1; add --new-lines-only and the same repo exits 0
#   in silence. The divergence is not exotic - it is `git add core && printf '' >
#   core`, the example the scanner's own header gives for why --staged exists.
#   Path-shaped findings (line 0) survive, which is likely why it went unnoticed.
#   FIX: --cached in the --staged branch.
#
#   A tracked .envrc carrying a licence-server export is never opened, because
#   the "extensionless" test is the glob `*.*`, which dotfiles match.
#
# deploy-gates.sh (746 lines) remains. Its fixture is the cost: a deploy manifest
# covering six time-ordered moments. Note before starting it: --selftest and
# --arm-only are claims each file makes about ITSELF. --arm-only turned out not
# to mean "Arm vendor only" at all - it means ARM EVERY RULE, firing each on its
# own specimen and scanning nothing. Read what a flag does before asserting it.
